// SOUNDNESS QUEUE row arraylit_partial_generic_enum_elems_internal (tier 3).
// AN ARRAY LITERAL WHOSE ELEMENTS EACH FIX ONLY PART OF A GENERIC ENUM'S
// ARGUMENTS IS REFUSED WITH AN INTERNAL ERROR. `[Result::Ok(1i64),
// Result::Err(true)]` — element 0 is `Result<i64, ?>`, element 1 is
// `Result<?, bool>`; rustc unifies them to `Result<i64, bool>`. logosc types
// the literal from element 0 and mlir-gen stops at
// "unknown tagged enum 'Result__i64__<error>'".
// With an annotation (`let v: [Result<i64, bool>; 2] = ...`) it compiles: each
// element takes the annotation as its expected type (lower_arr_lit /
// ElemHintScope). A NULLARY ctor before a concrete sibling (`[None,
// Some(1i64)]`) is re-lowered against the sibling's type, which is sound
// because the ctor is a pure literal. A payload ctor is not, so closing this
// row needs a unification of the partial argument lists and a lowering that
// does not evaluate the payload twice.
// Reason rowed (neighbour rule, reason 3): its own cost is unpriced. It was
// found beside the nullary-ctor inference fix, which cannot re-lower payloads.
package arraylit_partial_generic_enum_elems_internal;

fn main() -> i32 {
    let v = [Result::Ok(1i64), Result::Err(true)];
    match v[1] { Result::Err(b) => { if b { return 0i32; } return 2i32; } Result::Ok(_x) => { return 1i32; } }
}
